    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-d/q2",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q2-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The continuous random variable Y is Normally distributed with a mean of 122 and a standard deviation of 14. ( ) 4 a) Find ( P 125 &lt; Y &lt; 139 ) . b) Determine the value of a such that ( P 101 &lt; Y &lt; a ) = 0.8276 ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q2-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let Φ denote the standard normal cumulative distribution function. (a) Here Y ∼N(122, 142). Subtract the two lower-tail areas: P(Y &lt; 139) −P(Y &lt; 125). The corresponding z-scores are (139 −122)/14 = 1.2143 . . . and (125 −122)/14 = 0.2143 . . . .\" style=\"--madas-width:540.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q2-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Standardising gives P(125 &lt; Y &lt; 139) = Φ(17/14) −Φ(3/14) ≈0.88768 −0.58484 ≈0.3028 . [M1] Standardise the lower limit [M1] standardise the upper limit [A1] subtract the two cumulative probabilities [A1] evaluate (b) Begin with P(Y &lt; a) −P(Y &lt; 101) = 0.8276. In upper-tail form P(Y &lt; a) −[1 −P(Y &gt; 101)] = 0.8276; hence P(Y &lt; a) + P(Y &gt; 101) = 1.8276. The z-score is (101 −122)/14 = −1.5, giving P(Y &gt; 101) ≈0.9332. Since P(Y &lt; 101) = Φ(−1.5) ≈0.066807, the required lower-tail probability at a is\" style=\"--madas-width:644.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q2-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"0.8276 + 0.066807 = 0.894407 . . . . [B1] [M1] [A1] Inverting gives (a −122)/14 ≈1.25, so a −122 ≈17.5. Thus a = 122 + 14Φ−1(0.894407 . . .) ≈139.5 . [M1] [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 2 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The continuous random variable Y is Normally distributed with a mean of 122 and a standard deviation of 14. ( ) 4 a) Find ( P 125 < Y < 139 ) . b) Determine the value of a such that ( P 101 < Y < a ) = 0.8276 ( ) 6"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-d/q3",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The events A and B satisfy P ( ) A = 0.4 and         ( P A ∪ B ) = 0.79 . Determine ( ) P B in each of the two following cases. a) If A and B are mutually exclusive. ( ) 2 b) If A and B are independent. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:580.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The union formula is 0.79 = 0.4 + P(B) −P(A ∩B). (a) For mutually exclusive events the intersection probability is zero. Thus 0.79 = 0.4 + P(B) and 0.79 −0.4 = 0.39, giving P(B) = 0.39 . [M1] [A1] (b) For independent events P(A ∩B) = 0.4 P(B). [M1] Substitute in the union formula: 0.79 = 0.4 + P(B) −0.4 P(B). Subtract 0.4 and collect the terms in P(B). Thus 0.39 = 0.6 P(B) [A1] and P(B) = 0.65 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 5 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The events A and B satisfy P ( ) A = 0.4 and         ( P A ∪ B ) = 0.79 . Determine ( ) P B in each of the two following cases. a) If A and B are mutually exclusive. ( ) 2 b) If A and B are independent. ( ) 3"
  },
