  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-d/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"Taxis in Pajan have to pass an additional safety test consisting of three parts, one for the brakes, one for the tyres and one for the lights. A taxi must pass all three parts. The individual probabilities that a taxi will fail the “brake part”, the “tyre part” and the “light part” are 1 6 , 1 4 and 1 5 , respectively. These probabilities are independent of one another. A taxi from Pajan is tested at random. ( ) a) Find the probability that it will fail exactly one of the three parts of the test. 4 Safety regulations change so that the test has to be performed in the order “brake part” first, “tyre part” next and “lights part” last.\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"If the taxi fails one of the three parts the test results in failure, without any of remaining parts of the test having to be carried out. A taxi from Pajan is tested at random under these new regulations. b) Find the probability that it will fail the test. ( ) 4 c) Given a taxi failed the test, determine the probability it failed the “lights part”. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Using a tree diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:468.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Use pass probabilities 5/6, 3/4, 4/5 for brakes, tyres and lights: (a) Add the probabilities for failure in just one part: 1 3 4 5 + 5 1 5 + 5 4 3 1 5 = 1 10 + 1 6 + 1 6 4 6 4 6 4 8 = 12 + 20 + 15 120 47 = 120 . [A1] Only the first part fails [A1] only the second fails [A1] only the third fails [A1] add the disjoint products (b) Under the new regulations the fail paths are: brakes fail, brakes pass and tyres fail, or both pass and lights fail: [B1] [A1] The failed-test probability is 1 6 + 5 1 4 + 5 3 1 5 = 1 6 + 5 24 + 1 6 6 4 8 [M1] [A1] = 1 2 .\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"(c) As an alternative to summing the failure paths in (b), the only pass path has probability (5/6)(3/4)(4/5) = 1/2. Hence P(fail) = 1 −1/2 . = 1/2 A taxi can fail the lights part only after passing the earlier parts: [B1] Thus P(lights fail ∩test fails) = (5/6)(3/4)(1/5) . = 1/8 The probability of that path, divided by the probability of failure, is (1/8)/(1/2) = 1 4 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "Taxis in Pajan have to pass an additional safety test consisting of three parts, one for the brakes, one for the tyres and one for the lights. A taxi must pass all three parts. The individual probabilities that a taxi will fail the “brake part”, the “tyre part” and the “light part” are 1 6 , 1 4 and 1 5 , respectively. These probabilities are independent of one another. A taxi from Pajan is tested at random. ( ) a) Find the probability that it will fail exactly one of the three parts of the test. 4 Safety regulations change so that the test has to be performed in the order “brake part” first, “tyre part” next and “lights part” last. If the taxi fails one of the three parts the test results in failure, without any of remaining parts of the test having to be carried out. A taxi from Pajan is tested at random under these new regulations. b) Find the probability that it will fail the test. ( ) 4 c) Given a taxi failed the test, determine the probability it failed the “lights part”. ( ) 3"
