  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-d/q8",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q8-question-1.svg\" alt=\"It has been established over a long period of time that in a particular variety of rose bushes, 0.2 produce pink flowers. A selection of 10 rose bushes of this variety, are bought. a) Find probability that more than 4 of these bushes will produce red flowers. ( ) 2 b) Calculate the least number of rose bushes that need to be bought so that the probability of producing at least 1 plant with pink flowers exceeds 0.975. ( ) 4 Another selection of 125 rose bushes of this variety are bought. c) Use a Normal distribution approximation to determine the probability that the number of bushes which will produce pink flowers will be more than 21 but no more than 30. ( ) 7\" style=\"--madas-width:569.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q8-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Finally a selection of 25 rose bushes of the same variety are considered. When these rose bushes flowered they produced 10 plants with pink flowers. d) Stating your hypotheses clearly, test at the 1% level of significance, whether this constitutes evidence that this variety of rose bushes have a higher probability than 0.2 in producing pink flowers. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:572.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) The source uses n = 20, for which P(X ≥5) = 1 −P(X ≤4) ≈1 −0.6296 . = 0.3704 Using the ten bushes in the question, the same calculation gives the following:\" style=\"--madas-width:534.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"For X ∼B(10, 0.2), P(X &gt; 4) = 1 −P(X ≤4) ≈0.03279 . [M1] [A1] (b) For n bushes the probability of at least one pink-flowering plant is 1 −0.8n. [M1] The target is P(X ≥1) &gt; 0.975, or 1 −P(X = 0) &gt; 0.975. n (0.2)0(0.8)n, require 0.8n &lt; 0.025, [A1] hence n log(0.8) &lt; log(0.025). With P(X = 0) = 0 Dividing by a negative logarithm gives n &gt; log(0.025)/ log(0.8) ≈16.53. [A1] The minimum is 17 . [A1] (c) For 125 bushes, X ∼B(125, 0.2), [B1] [A1] with mean 125(0.2) = 25 and variance 25(0.8) = 20. [M1] [A1] Use Y ∼N(25, 20) [M1] and P(21 &lt; X ≤30) = P(22 ≤X ≤30). The source writes the normal area as P(Y &lt; 30.5) + P(Y &gt; 21.5) −1, approximately Φ(1.23) + Φ(0.78) −1.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q8-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Equivalently, apply the continuity correction:  5.5 −3.5   √ √ P(22 ≤X ≤30) ≈P(21.5 &lt; Y &lt; 30.5) = Φ −Φ ≈0.6737 . [M1] [A1] 20 20 (d) Let p be the probability that a bush produces pink flowers: [M1] Test H0 : p = 0.2 [A1] against H1 : p &gt; 0.2. [A1] For X ∼B(25, 0.2), P(X ≥10) = 1 −P(X ≤9) ≈1 −0.98267 . [A1] = 0.01733 &gt; 0.01 Fail to reject H0. [A1] There is insufficient evidence at the 1% level that p &gt; 0.2. [A1] Source note. Part (a) uses the question’s sample size of ten: The handwritten calculation uses twenty bushes.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 8 is 19 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "It has been established over a long period of time that in a particular variety of rose bushes, 0.2 produce pink flowers. A selection of 10 rose bushes of this variety, are bought. a) Find probability that more than 4 of these bushes will produce red flowers. ( ) 2 b) Calculate the least number of rose bushes that need to be bought so that the probability of producing at least 1 plant with pink flowers exceeds 0.975. ( ) 4 Another selection of 125 rose bushes of this variety are bought. c) Use a Normal distribution approximation to determine the probability that the number of bushes which will produce pink flowers will be more than 21 but no more than 30. ( ) 7 Finally a selection of 25 rose bushes of the same variety are considered. When these rose bushes flowered they produced 10 plants with pink flowers. d) Stating your hypotheses clearly, test at the 1% level of significance, whether this constitutes evidence that this variety of rose bushes have a higher probability than 0.2 in producing pink flowers. ( ) 6"
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-d/q9",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q9-question-1.svg\" alt=\"3 m A B C 8 m A non uniform plank of wood AB has length 8 m and mass 100 kg . The plank is smoothly supported at its two ends A and B . A boy of mass 60 kg\" style=\"--madas-width:556.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q9-question-2.svg\" alt=\"stands on the plank at the point C , where AC = 3 m , as shown in the figure above. The plank with the boy standing on the plank, remains in equilibrium with AB horizontal. The plank is modelled as a non uniform rod and the boy as a particle. a) Given that the reactions at the two supports are equal, determine the distance of the centre of mass of the plank from A . ( ) 4 b) Explain in the context of this problem the model of i. … the plank is a rod ( ) 1 ii. … the boy is a particle. ( ) 1\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q9-question-3.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:568.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Start with a diagram: (a) The total downward force is 160g, so vertical equilibrium gives R + R = 60g + 100g, or 2R = 160g. Thus the two equal reactions are 80g each: [B1] Let x be the distance of the plank’s centre of mass from A. [A1] Moments about A give\" style=\"--madas-width:669.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q9-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"80g(8) = 60g(3) + 100gx, 180g + 100gx = 640g, 100gx = 460g, x = 4.6 m . [M1] [A1] (b) (i) The plank can be treated as a rigid line with negligible width and thickness: Its weight acts at its centre of mass: [M1] (ii) The boy’s dimensions are negligible for this model: His entire weight acts at C. [M1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
