      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q16-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Take the positive horizontal direction along the F force and resolve downwards: [M1] The 30-newton force points below that direction at 150◦. [A1] The resultant is below the F force at 60◦. [A1] R sin 60◦= 30 sin 150◦= 15, R cos 60◦= F + 30 cos 150◦. [A1] √ √ The vertical equation reads 30/2 = R 3/2, hence 30 = R 3. The horizontal equation reads √ F −15 3 = R/2 √ . = 5 3 Thus √ R = 10 3 ≈17.3 [A1] √ and F = 20 3 ≈34.6 , in newtons: [A1] Alternative by the force triangle. The right triangle gives tan 30◦= R/30, so\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-d/q16-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"R = 30 tan 30◦ √ . = 10 3 Also cos 30◦= 30/F, hence F = 30/ cos 30◦ √ . = 20 3\" style=\"--madas-width:392.0px\"></figure>",
